btrfs: no preemptive metadata reclaim, and lend half the free device to a reservation

Preemptive reclaim flushes delayed allocation before any metadata shortage,
so that no reservation has to wait for a flush. Its trigger is the size of
the reservations dirty data holds, and a package tree of 75,000 small files
holds a reservation per file while it is being copied — well over the 128 MB
that arms it, on a filesystem with tens of gigabytes unallocated. Every copy
of such a tree was written back while it was still being written, one bio
per file, with the flush's workers waking each other across every idle CPU:
2.9 s of system time for a 1 GB copy that costs 1.4 s with the writeback
left to the dirty flusher, and a copy OrbStack's guest finishes in 1.1 s.

`btrfs.preemptive_reclaim` (default off) is the switch. The on-demand
reclaim that runs when a reservation actually fails is untouched, so nothing
waits longer for space than it would have; it only stops being flushed early.

`btrfs.overcommit_shift` (default 1, half the unallocated device; upstream's
3 is an eighth) is how much a flushing reservation may borrow against. The
same tree of small files that arms preemptive reclaim also outruns that
eighth, and each reservation past it is a ticket, a flush of the data being
written, and the writer blocked behind it.

Measured on the 1 GB tree copy in a live guest, 64 GiB disk: reclaim on,
1.9–2.3 s and 2.9 s of system time; reclaim off, 1.9 s and 2.4; with half
the device lent as well, 1.5–1.7 s and the flush coalesces from 47,000
writes to 8,600. With the guest at 16 GB, the size OrbStack's is on that
machine, 1.1–1.2 s — theirs is 1.05–1.3.

--- a/fs/btrfs/space-info.c
+++ b/fs/btrfs/space-info.c
@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
 
 #include <linux/spinlock.h>
+#include <linux/moduleparam.h>
 #include <linux/minmax.h>
 #include "misc.h"
 #include "ctree.h"
@@ -207,6 +208,11 @@
 /*
  * Calculate chunk size depending on volume type (regular or zoned).
  */
+static unsigned int overcommit_shift = 1;
+module_param(overcommit_shift, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(overcommit_shift,
+		 "Lend 1/2^n of the unallocated device to a flushing metadata reservation (default 1; upstream 3)");
+
 static u64 calc_chunk_size(const struct btrfs_fs_info *fs_info, u64 flags)
 {
 	if (btrfs_is_zoned(fs_info))
@@ -475,8 +481,17 @@
 	 * 1/2th of the space. If we can flush, don't let us overcommit
 	 * too much, let it overcommit up to 1/8 of the space.
 	 */
+	/*
+	 * How much of the unallocated device a reservation may borrow against.
+	 * Upstream lends an eighth to a flushing reservation and half to one
+	 * that cannot flush; a tree of small files holds a worst-case
+	 * reservation per file while it is being written, so 75,000 files ask
+	 * for 19 GB and an eighth of a 60 GB device is 7 — every one past
+	 * that is a ticket, a flush of the data being written, and the writer
+	 * blocked behind it. `btrfs.overcommit_shift` is measured, not chosen.
+	 */
 	if (flush == BTRFS_RESERVE_FLUSH_ALL)
-		avail >>= 3;
+		avail >>= overcommit_shift;
 	else
 		avail >>= 1;
 
@@ -928,6 +943,21 @@
 	return to_reclaim;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Preemptive reclaim flushes delayed allocation ahead of any shortage, so
+ * that a reservation never has to wait for a flush. Its trigger is the
+ * amount of metadata reserved by dirty data, and a tree of small files
+ * reserves gigabytes while it is being written — so on a filesystem with
+ * most of its device unallocated, it still flushes the whole tree while the
+ * copy is in progress, one bio per file, and its workers wake each other
+ * across every idle CPU. The reservations were never going to fail. The
+ * on-demand reclaim, which runs when one actually does, is untouched.
+ */
+static bool preemptive_reclaim;
+module_param(preemptive_reclaim, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(preemptive_reclaim,
+		 "Flush delayed allocation ahead of a metadata shortage (default off; upstream on)");
+
 static bool need_preemptive_reclaim(struct btrfs_fs_info *fs_info,
 				    const struct btrfs_space_info *space_info)
 {
@@ -936,6 +966,9 @@
 	u64 thresh;
 	u64 used;
 
+	if (!preemptive_reclaim)
+		return false;
+
 	thresh = mult_perc(space_info->total_bytes, 90);
 
 	lockdep_assert_held(&space_info->lock);
